            <ArticleTitle>[Assessment of disease control among asthmatic adolescents].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>859-66</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">/S0034-98872008000700006</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">The clinical assessment of asthma control is fundamental to evaluate the results of treatment. The Asthma Control Test (ACT) is a questionnaire with five scored items, that differentiates between a well controlled or uncontrolled asthma. It has a Spanish version and has been applied in different countries.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To assess asthma control using the ACT and compare it with the clinical assessment of specialists in a group of pediatric patients.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIAL AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The ACT was applied to 220 adolescents aged 12 to 17 years, 54% males, with persistent asthma, attended at a respiratory disease unit of a pediatric hospital. The concordance between ACT results and the assessment of specialists was also evaluated.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">According to ACT and specialists, asthma was controlled in 54% and 63% of patients, respectively. There was a weak concordance between ACT and specialists assessment (Kappa index: 0.27; 95% confidence intervals: 0.14-0.4). The degree of asthma control decreased along with increasing severity of the disease (chi2 = 10.128, p =0.001).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Half of the evaluated asthmatic adolescents do not have an adequate control of their disease. Severity of the disease is inversely related to the degree of control.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
